# NubArca production environment variables.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` next to docker-compose.prod.yml and edit. The real
# `.env` is gitignored — do NOT commit secrets.
#
# Usage:
#   cp .env.example .env
#   $EDITOR .env                        # fill in real values
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml --env-file .env up -d
#
# All variable names use docker-compose's default dotenv interpolation. Names
# with double underscores (`__`) map to nested ASP.NET Core configuration
# keys (e.g. `ConnectionStrings__Postgres` → `ConnectionStrings:Postgres`).
#
# docker-compose.prod.yml passes every key documented here through to the api
# container with the SAME safe default shown below, so a commented-out key
# keeps the conservative default and uncommenting it takes effect on the next
# `up -d`. Optional toggles (startup-migrate, storage limits, FFmpeg posters,
# the background-job worker, cleanup services) are all off / unlimited by
# default — see deploy/FIRST_DEPLOY.md step 9 "Optional features".

# === PostgreSQL ====================================================
POSTGRES_DB=nubarca
POSTGRES_USER=nubarca
# Required. Generate with e.g.   openssl rand -base64 32
# Use a long random string. Never reuse from a sample / documentation.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=CHANGE-ME-TO-A-STRONG-RANDOM-PASSWORD

# Backend connection string. The host `postgres` resolves to the compose
# service. Username + password MUST match POSTGRES_USER / POSTGRES_PASSWORD
# above; database name MUST match POSTGRES_DB.
ConnectionStrings__Postgres=Host=postgres;Port=5432;Database=nubarca;Username=nubarca;Password=CHANGE-ME-TO-A-STRONG-RANDOM-PASSWORD

# === ASP.NET Core ==================================================
ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production

# === Database migrations ==========================================
# Off by default: the API does NOT touch the schema on startup. The supported
# path is the operator CLI, which gives you a back-up window before any DDL
# fires:
#
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml --env-file .env run --rm api \
#     dotnet NubArca.Api.dll db migrate
#
# Set to true to apply pending EF Core migrations automatically on every API
# start. Useful on small single-replica deploys where you don't want to
# remember the manual step. NOT recommended for multi-replica setups: EF Core
# has no built-in cross-instance migration lock, so a rolling restart can
# race two replicas into the same MigrateAsync(). Always back up the database
# before flipping this flag against a populated deployment.
# Database__MigrateOnStartup=false

# === Blob storage =================================================
# Path INSIDE the api container for ORIGINAL blobs. The `storage-data` named
# volume mounts here (see docker-compose.prod.yml). Derived artifacts
# (thumbnails / previews / posters) also live here UNLESS you set
# Storage__DerivedRootPath (slice 72) to split them onto a separate root.
Storage__RootPath=/var/lib/nubarca/storage

# --- Single-server storage layout (slice 71) ---------------------
# HOST paths used ONLY when you switch the compose volumes to bind mounts
# (uncomment the driver_opts blocks in docker-compose.prod.yml). Default
# deployments use Docker-managed named volumes and can ignore this block.
# See deploy/FIRST_DEPLOY.md "Storage layout" for the mount steps.
#
# The values below are the RECOMMENDED layout for the production server:
# PostgreSQL on the fast root filesystem, everything large + regenerable on
# the RAID1 array. Copy these into your real .env and uncomment.
#
# Tier 1 — fast root disk: PostgreSQL data.
#   Why: PostgreSQL is latency-sensitive (random I/O for indexes, WAL fsync);
#   it benefits most from the fastest disk. `/srv/...` is a plain directory on
#   the existing root filesystem — no separate mount needed, just `mkdir -p`.
# NUBARCA_POSTGRES_DATA=/srv/nubarca/postgres
#
# Tier 3 — RAID1 array: original blob storage (the Storage__RootPath volume's
# host backing).
#   Why: original files are large and grow without bound; they belong on the
#   roomy, redundant RAID1 disk, NOT on the small root filesystem.
# NUBARCA_BLOB_DATA=/mnt/raid1/nubarca/blobs
#
# Tier 3 — RAID1 array: local backups.
#   Why: backups must NOT live only on the database disk — a disk failure
#   would take the data AND its backup. Keep them on the separate RAID1 array
#   (and still ship copies off-host). backup.sh reads BACKUP_DIR (arg 1
#   overrides), so either export it or pass the path explicitly.
# BACKUP_DIR=/mnt/raid1/nubarca/backups
#
# Tier 2 — derived artifacts (thumbnails, medium previews, video posters).
#   WIRED as of slice 72. Maps to Storage__DerivedRootPath. When set, derived
#   artifacts are stored/read under this root instead of the blob root; when
#   unset they stay in the blob store (single-root default, unchanged). Derived
#   artifacts are REGENERABLE cache, not source data — this disk needs neither
#   the redundancy nor the backups the originals do, and can be a faster cache
#   disk. To activate: set this, set Storage__DerivedRootPath to the matching
#   in-container path, and uncomment the storage-derived-data volume + mount in
#   docker-compose.prod.yml. On the current server (no separate cache disk yet)
#   leaving this unset keeps derived artifacts on RAID1 with the originals.
# NUBARCA_DERIVED_DATA=/mnt/media-cache/nubarca/derived
# Storage__DerivedRootPath=/var/lib/nubarca/derived   # in-container path for the derived mount
#
# MONITOR ROOT FREE SPACE. Because PostgreSQL sits on the root filesystem
# (~35 GB on this server), watch it: WAL + table growth on a full root disk
# can wedge the whole host (Docker, logs, the DB itself). Alert well before
# it fills — e.g. `df -h /` in your monitor, or a cron that warns under ~5 GB.

# Slice 65 — app-level per-file upload ceiling (bytes), enforced WHILE
# streaming to disk (never buffers the whole file). 0 or unset = no app-level
# limit. Default 2 GiB (2147483648). This is SEPARATE from the reverse-proxy /
# Kestrel request-body limit, which you must set independently on nginx/Caddy
# (e.g. nginx `client_max_body_size`) — the proxy limit governs the raw HTTP
# body, this one governs the decoded file stream.
# Storage__MaxUploadBytes=2147483648

# === Upload size limits (slice 78) ================================
# Slice 78: Kestrel and ASP.NET Core FormOptions limits. Enforced by the
# framework BEFORE the app touches the request body. Default 10 GiB.
# The old Kestrel default was 30 MB — too small for video uploads, causing
# "Request body too large" errors that the app couldn't catch gracefully.
#
# Set both to the same value. Neither replaces the reverse-proxy limit
# (nginx client_max_body_size / Caddy request_body max_size) — all three
# must agree for large uploads to work end-to-end.
# Uploads__MaxRequestBodySizeBytes=10737418240
# Uploads__MaxFileSizeBytes=10737418240

# === Party public links/uploads ==================================
# Production should set a stable random token secret so public party/view-upload
# links remain signed by a deployment-specific secret instead of the app fallback.
# Generate with e.g.   openssl rand -base64 32
# Party__TokenSecret=CHANGE-ME-TO-A-STRONG-RANDOM-SECRET
#
# Per-file caps for anonymous party uploads, per MEDIA KIND. Images and videos
# are separate on purpose: raising one number for both would silently grant a
# half-gigabyte allowance to every photo upload.
#
# Party__MaxUploadBytes is the historical, photo-oriented key and still governs
# IMAGES, so an installation that already set it keeps exactly the behaviour it
# configured. Party__MaxImageUploadBytes is the explicit new name and wins when
# both are set. Defaults: 50 MiB images, 512 MiB videos.
# Party__MaxUploadBytes=52428800
# Party__MaxImageUploadBytes=52428800
# Party__MaxVideoUploadBytes=536870912
#
# The EFFECTIVE ceiling is the SMALLEST of these layers, so a 512 MiB video only
# actually arrives if every one of them allows it:
#   reverse proxy      nginx client_max_body_size   (example config: 1G)
#   Kestrel            Uploads__MaxRequestBodySizeBytes   (default 10 GiB)
#   ASP.NET multipart  Uploads__MaxFileSizeBytes          (default 10 GiB)
#   app-layer stream   Storage__MaxUploadBytes            (default 2 GiB)
#   party per-kind     the two keys above
# The reverse proxy is the first thing to hit and the easiest to forget: with
# the example 1G it is fine, but lowering it below the party video cap truncates
# the request before any of these settings are consulted.
#
# Public face search remains local to the guest device by default. Enabling TV
# activation makes one album-wide filter visible to every paired TV on that
# same Party album; opt in only when that shared behavior is wanted.
# Party__FaceSearch__TvActivationEnabled=false

# === Plates (Targhe) — owner-private license-plate image surface =========
# Production should set a stable random pepper so the hidden per-owner logical
# container key (__nubarca_plates_{ownerScopedHash}) is not derivable from the
# owner id alone. MUST be stable across restarts (a changed pepper produces a
# different container key for the same owner). Unset = built-in dev fallback.
# Generate with e.g.   openssl rand -base64 32
# Plates__Pepper=CHANGE-ME-TO-A-STRONG-RANDOM-SECRET
#
# Per-file cap for plate uploads (bytes). Also bounded by Storage__MaxUploadBytes
# and the global request-body/proxy limits. Default 25 MiB.
# Plates__MaxUploadBytes=26214400
#
# Video semantic AI is opt-in. Enable both only after the image-embedding
# profile and FFmpeg worker path have been validated; segmentation creates the
# temporal manifest, visual embeddings process its representative frames.
# Ai__VideoSegmentation__Enabled=false
# Ai__VideoVisualEmbeddings__Enabled=false
#
# Canonical video FACE TRACKS. Independent of the visual embeddings above (it
# needs the temporal manifest only) and of the photo face flags. It produces
# blob-level evidence — timestamps and aggregate face vectors — and NEVER any
# person identity. Enable only after segmentation and the face package have been
# validated: it samples ~1 frame/second and runs detection + recognition on each.
# Bump AnalysisVersion to reanalyse; the previous tracks are kept alongside.
# Ai__VideoFaceAnalysis__Enabled=false
# Ai__VideoFaceAnalysis__AnalysisVersion=1
# Ai__VideoFaceAnalysis__FrameIntervalMilliseconds=1000
# Ai__VideoFaceAnalysis__MaximumFramesPerVideo=900
#
# Face-analysis frame resolution, INDEPENDENT of
# Ai__VideoVisualEmbeddings__FrameMaxEdge: raising it makes distant faces
# detectable (MinimumFaceSizePixels is measured against this frame) and makes
# every frame more expensive. Range [640, 8192]; 640 is the detector input edge.
# Ai__VideoFaceAnalysis__FrameMaxEdge=768
#
# Plates ALPR (license-plate recognition) pipeline. SEPARATE from Ai__Face* and
# People — no identity model is shared. Disabled by default. Provider selects the
# runner: Disabled | DeterministicDev (non-semantic dev/test) | Onnx (real
# in-process ONNX detector+OCR; requires model files, none committed). When
# Provider is empty it falls back to the legacy Enabled bool (Enabled=true →
# DeterministicDev). Production MUST NOT set Provider=Onnx until real model files
# exist AND `plates models validate alpr` passes. See docs/model-deployment/plates.md.
# Plates__Alpr__Enabled=false
# Plates__Alpr__Provider=Disabled
# Plates__Alpr__ProfileKey=plate-alpr-v1
# Plates__Alpr__DetectorModelPath=
# Plates__Alpr__DetectorModelKind=Yolo
# Plates__Alpr__DetectorInputWidth=640
# Plates__Alpr__DetectorInputHeight=640
# Plates__Alpr__DetectorConfidenceThreshold=0.35
# Plates__Alpr__DetectorNmsThreshold=0.45
# Plates__Alpr__OcrModelPath=
# Plates__Alpr__OcrModelKind=FastPlateOcr
# Plates__Alpr__OcrInputWidth=160
# Plates__Alpr__OcrInputHeight=40
# Plates__Alpr__OcrAlphabet=0123456789ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ
# Plates__Alpr__MinPlateConfidence=0.35
# Plates__Alpr__MinOcrConfidence=0.30
# Plates__Alpr__MaxImagePixels=25000000
# Plates__Alpr__MaxDetectionsPerImage=32
# Plates__Alpr__WorkerConcurrency=1
#
# Plates PRIVACY-ONLY face redaction (blur/pixelate detected faces in served
# plate media). SEPARATE from Ai__Face*, People, and Party__FaceSearch* — it is
# NOT identity: it creates no embeddings/clusters/persons and no cross-owner
# data. Disabled by default. Provider selects the detector: Disabled |
# DeterministicDev (non-semantic dev/test) | ExistingNubArcaFaceDetector
# (REUSES the AI substrate's face-box detector for BOUNDING BOXES ONLY — no
# embeddings/clusters/people) | OnnxDedicatedFaceDetector (future, not
# implemented). When Provider is empty it falls back to Enabled (Enabled=true →
# DeterministicDev). Enabled is the master switch. When unavailable, blurFaces=true
# returns a safe "not configured" error — it NEVER serves the unredacted image.
# For ExistingNubArcaFaceDetector the real face model is configured under
# Ai__Onnx__* (see docs/model-deployment/plates.md); leave
# ExistingDetectorProfileKey empty to use the default face profile.
# Plates__FaceRedaction__Enabled=false
# Plates__FaceRedaction__Provider=Disabled
# Plates__FaceRedaction__ProfileKey=plate-face-redaction-v1
# Plates__FaceRedaction__ExistingDetectorProfileKey=
# Plates__FaceRedaction__DetectorModelPath=
# Plates__FaceRedaction__MinConfidence=0.35
# Plates__FaceRedaction__NmsThreshold=0.45
# Plates__FaceRedaction__BoxExpansionRatio=0.35
# Plates__FaceRedaction__Mode=aggressive_pixelation
# Plates__FaceRedaction__PixelBlockSize=32
# Plates__FaceRedaction__MaxImagePixels=25000000
# Plates__FaceRedaction__MaxFacesPerImage=64
# Plates__FaceRedaction__CacheEnabled=true

# ── Aesthetics Lab (Laboratorio estetico) + HumanAesExpert sidecar ──────────
# Owner-private, OPT-IN, EXPERIMENTAL space for local human-image aesthetic
# analysis with KwaiVGI/HumanAesExpert-1B (MIT). DISABLED by default: lab
# browsing/upload still work, but "Start analysis" returns a controlled
# unavailable response and enqueues NOTHING until Enabled=true AND a reachable
# internal sidecar is configured. The model NEVER analyzes the whole gallery —
# only images explicitly added to the lab, on explicit request. Weights are
# never committed; install them with scripts/install-human-aesexpert-model.sh
# and run the sidecar via docker-compose.human-aesexpert.yml. See
# docs/model-deployment/human-aesexpert.md.
# HumanAesExpert__Enabled=false
# HumanAesExpert__ProfileKey=human-aesexpert-1b-expert-v1
# HumanAesExpert__DefaultCapabilities=expert_scores
# Per-capability gates. Only expert_scores is validated/enabled in this slice;
# the other three are prepared but OFF until separately benchmarked.
# HumanAesExpert__AllowExpertScores=true
# HumanAesExpert__AllowScoreHead=false
# HumanAesExpert__AllowMetaVoter=false
# HumanAesExpert__AllowTextAssessment=false
# HumanAesExpert__MaximumBatchItems=20
# Conservative per-inference timeout (the 1B model on CPU can be slow).
# HumanAesExpert__RequestTimeoutSeconds=120
# Internal-only sidecar URL (Docker internal network; no public port). Empty ⇒
# the feature is effectively unavailable.
# HumanAesExpert__SidecarBaseUrl=http://human-aesexpert:8091
# official-v1 preserves the checkpoint's own preprocessing (448x448 dynamic
# tiling). A reduced pipeline MUST use a DIFFERENT profile key.
# HumanAesExpert__PreprocessingProfileKey=human-aesexpert-official-v1
# HumanAesExpert__MaxUploadBytes=26214400
# Stable pepper for the hidden per-owner container key
# (__nubarca_aesthetics_{ownerScopedHash}); unset = built-in dev fallback.
# HumanAesExpert__Pepper=CHANGE-ME-TO-A-STRONG-RANDOM-SECRET
# Sidecar-only resource tuning (host topology specific). Empty CPUSET means no
# affinity. Example for i7-12650H: one logical CPU from each of its six P-cores.
# HUMANAES_CPUSET=0,2,4,6,8,10
# HUMANAES_CPU_LIMIT=6.0
# HUMANAES_MEMORY_LIMIT=10g
# HUMANAES_TORCH_THREADS=6
# HUMANAES_TORCH_INTEROP_THREADS=1

# Slice 65 — default per-user logical-storage quota (bytes). Counts the sum of
# each user's owned FileItem.SizeBytes, INCLUDING trashed-but-not-purged files
# (permanent delete frees the space). A duplicate upload still counts because
# the user owns a logical file. Derived thumbnails/previews/posters do NOT
# count. 0 or unset = unlimited (pre-slice-65 behaviour). No admin exemption.
# Storage__DefaultUserQuotaBytes=0

# === Reverse-proxy / Forwarded Headers ==================G==========
# Enable when the api sits behind a reverse proxy that terminates TLS.
# The middleware rewrites Request.Scheme to "https" from X-Forwarded-Proto,
# which is required for the CSRF origin check to work correctly (a mismatch
# between "https" in Origin and "http" in the effective URL → 403).
ForwardedHeaders__Enabled=true
ForwardedHeaders__ForwardLimit=1

# === Password recovery by email (opt-in, OFF by default) ============
#
# With Mail__Enabled=false NOTHING about authentication changes: sign-in works
# exactly as before, the forgot-password page explains that email recovery is
# unavailable on this installation, and the administrator's manual password
# reset (Admin -> Users -> Security) remains the recovery path. Turning it on is
# an operator decision that needs REAL SMTP values — there are no defaults here,
# because a default SMTP host would be somebody else's server.
#
# The reset link's origin is NUBARCA_PUBLIC_ORIGIN (below), never the incoming
# Host header, and the token travels in the URL FRAGMENT so it cannot appear in
# a reverse-proxy access log.
#
# NEVER commit a real SMTP password. This file is an example; the values live in
# the operator's own .env.
Mail__Enabled=false
Mail__Smtp__Host=
Mail__Smtp__Port=587
Mail__Smtp__Username=
Mail__Smtp__Password=
# STARTTLS on 587, implicit TLS on 465 (chosen from the port). Only set false
# for a relay on the same host.
Mail__Smtp__UseTls=true
Mail__FromAddress=
Mail__FromName=NubArca
# How long a reset link lives. Short by design: it is a bearer credential
# sitting in a mailbox.
Mail__TokenLifetimeMinutes=30
# Per normalized email address, on top of the per-IP limiter below. An IP limit
# alone lets a distributed caller hammer one mailbox; an email limit alone lets
# one host walk an address list.
Mail__PerEmailPermitLimit=3
Mail__PerEmailWindowMinutes=15
# Per source IP for the public recovery endpoints. Conservative values suited to
# a self-hosted installation: a person retrying is fine, a script is not.
RateLimits__PasswordRecovery__PermitLimit=5
RateLimits__PasswordRecovery__WindowSeconds=300

# === DOCKER BRIDGE NETWORK — required when using Apache/nginx on the HOST ===
#
# The API container sees requests coming from the Docker bridge gateway IP
# (e.g. 172.18.0.1), NOT from 127.0.0.1. Without trusting that network the
# ForwardedHeaders middleware ignores X-Forwarded-Proto, the request looks
# like plain "http://", and the CSRF middleware returns 403 on every login.
#
# RECOMMENDED: trust only the Docker bridge subnet (not the whole internet).
# Find your subnet: docker network inspect nubarca-internal \
#   --format '{{range .IPAM.Config}}{{.Subnet}}{{end}}'
# Then set it here (typical Docker default is 172.17.0.0/16 or 172.18.0.0/16):
# ForwardedHeaders__KnownNetworks__0=172.18.0.0/16
#
# ALTERNATIVE — safe ONLY when ports are loopback-bound (127.0.0.1:8080/8081):
# Since the API is unreachable from outside except through the local proxy,
# TrustAny=true cannot be exploited by external attackers. Still, the
# KnownNetworks approach above is more precise and preferred.
# ForwardedHeaders__TrustAny=true

# Other trusted proxies / networks (load balancers, additional hops):
# If traffic really flows through multiple trusted proxy hops such as
# Apache -> nginx -> api, ForwardedHeaders__ForwardLimit=1 only consumes the
# immediately-prior hop from X-Forwarded-For. Raise ForwardLimit to the number
# of trusted hops only when those hops are explicitly trusted via KnownProxies /
# KnownNetworks (or the narrowly-scoped TrustAny fallback above).
# ForwardedHeaders__KnownProxies__0=10.0.0.5
# ForwardedHeaders__KnownNetworks__1=10.0.0.0/8

# === Rate limits ===================================================
# Defaults: 10 login attempts / 60s/IP; 60 public share-link hits / 60s/IP.
# Tighten if you see abusive traffic. State is in-process — not shared across
# replicas.
# RateLimits__Login__PermitLimit=10
# RateLimits__Login__WindowSeconds=60
# RateLimits__Share__PermitLimit=60
# RateLimits__Share__WindowSeconds=60
#
# Public party JSON/actions and public image download stay under the tight
# party policy. Thumbnail/preview derived media uses the higher PartyMedia
# policy so large public albums do not exhaust the JSON/download bucket.
# RateLimits__Party__PermitLimit=300
# RateLimits__Party__WindowSeconds=60
# RateLimits__Party__QueueLimit=0
# RateLimits__PartyMedia__PermitLimit=3000
# RateLimits__PartyMedia__WindowSeconds=60
# RateLimits__PartyMedia__QueueLimit=0
#
# TV Personal Area PIN unlock (and owner-side PIN creation): per-IP window on
# top of the per-session progressive cooldown (5 free failures, then 30s
# doubling per failure, capped at 15 min — persisted on the TV session).
# RateLimits__TvPersonalUnlock__PermitLimit=10
# RateLimits__TvPersonalUnlock__WindowSeconds=60

# === Cleanup background services (opt-in, OFF by default) =========
# Enable BOTH together to actually reclaim deleted file rows + their blobs.
# The windows are sequential and independent: FileItemSweeper grace starts
# when a file enters Trash; BlobJanitor grace starts only after the retained
# FileItem row (or another final owner) is permanently removed. 1440 = 24h.
# Example: 10-day Trash + 1-hour physical-blob safety window:
# FileItemSweeper__GraceMinutes=14400
# BlobJanitor__GraceMinutes=60
# FileItemSweeper__Enabled=true
# FileItemSweeper__IntervalMinutes=5
# FileItemSweeper__GraceMinutes=1440
# BlobJanitor__Enabled=true
# BlobJanitor__IntervalMinutes=5
# BlobJanitor__GraceMinutes=1440

# === Image processing caps (defaults are reasonable) ==============
# ImageProcessing__EnableThumbnails=true
# ImageProcessing__MaxWidth=8192
# ImageProcessing__MaxHeight=8192
# ImageProcessing__MaxPixels=64000000
# ImageProcessing__MaxThumbnailInputBytes=31457280

# === Image derivative backend (slice 100) ===========================
# Thumbnails/previews render via libvips (bundled — no apt packages) with
# automatic fallback to the pure-managed ImageSharp pipeline. libvips is
# ~2-3x faster on typical photos (shrink-on-load). Defaults are production-
# ready; override only to tune or to force a backend. Compare on your data:
#   dotnet NubArca.Api.dll media derivatives benchmark --limit 50
#
# ImageBackend: auto (prefer vips when available) | vips | imagesharp
# MediaDerivatives__ImageBackend=auto
# MediaDerivatives__FallbackToImageSharp=true
# MediaDerivatives__VipsEnabled=true
# MediaDerivatives__VipsConcurrency=0          # 0 = #cores; lower on small hosts
# MediaDerivatives__SmallQuality=80
# MediaDerivatives__MediumQuality=80
# MediaDerivatives__SmallMaxEdge=768
# MediaDerivatives__MediumPreviewMaxEdge=1920
# MediaDerivatives__PosterWidth=1280
# MediaDerivatives__PosterHeight=720
# MediaDerivatives__VideoPreviewFrameWidth=480
# MediaDerivatives__VideoPreviewFrameHeight=270
# MediaDerivatives__RenderTimeoutSeconds=30

# === Video poster/preview providers ==================================
# The production image bundles ffmpeg + ffprobe. Keep these values aligned on
# API and worker so forced gallery regeneration emits real frames and legacy
# video containers are classified before traversal.
#
# Media__VideoPosterProvider=ffmpeg
# Media__FfmpegPath=ffmpeg
# Media__VideoMetadataProvider=ffprobe
# Media__FfprobePath=ffprobe
# Media__VideoPosterTimeoutSeconds=10
# Media__VideoPreviewStripTimeoutSeconds=45
# Media__VideoPosterSeekSeconds=3
# Media__VideoPosterMaxOutputBytes=20971520

# === Google Cast (docs/google-cast.md) ===============================
# Sending a video to a Chromecast / Google TV / Android TV. Gated by the
# `cast.access` permission; a receiver plays through a SHORT-LIVED, single-video
# grant, never through the owner's session.
#
# How long a delegated playback capability stays valid when nothing revokes it.
# It is the backstop for a browser that vanished mid-cast: long enough for a
# film, short enough that a leaked URL is not an open door. Clamped to
# 30–720 minutes; a value outside that range is corrected, not obeyed.
Cast__GrantLifetimeMinutes=360
#
# The EXACT origin a Cast receiver presents. There is no default and no
# wildcard: `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` on a URL that carries a bearer
# secret would let any page on the internet read protected media. Leave it empty
# until the value has been OBSERVED from a real device during the first physical
# cast — a guess here is worse than an empty list, because an empty list simply
# advertises nothing and grant creation still works.
#
# The observed value is INSTALLATION-SPECIFIC production configuration and is
# deliberately not written into this template. Capture it from the sanitized
# Cast access log (see docs/google-cast.md) and set it here, exactly, with no
# trailing slash.
Cast__AllowedReceiverOrigins__0=
#
# Grant CREATION is capped per user (segment fetches deliberately are not — one
# film is hundreds of them, and the bearer URL is already scoped and expiring).
# RateLimits__CastGrantCreate__PermitLimit=20
# RateLimits__CastGrantCreate__WindowSeconds=60
#
# A Cast media URL carries its secret in the query string, so a reverse proxy
# logging $request would write it to disk. deploy/nginx.conf.example ships a
# token-free log format for /api/cast/media/ — apply the same change to an
# operator-local proxy and verify with `grep -c 'token=' <access log>`.

# === Background jobs (slice 70 / hardened slice 89) ==================
# Durable DB-backed job queue (no Redis/RabbitMQ). The in-process worker
# is OFF by default — NubArca never processes jobs automatically unless
# Jobs__WorkerEnabled=true. Out-of-band alternatives: the operator CLI
# `dotnet NubArca.Api.dll jobs run-once` (one batch) / `jobs worker`
# (loop), or the optional dedicated `worker` compose service (start with
# `--profile worker`). Enqueue with `jobs enqueue <job>`; inspect with
# `jobs list`. Job payloads carry operation flags only — never paths,
# storage keys, raw metadata, or tokens.
#
# Lease/heartbeat (slice 89): a claimed job is owned for LeaseSeconds and
# heartbeated every HeartbeatSeconds (keep Heartbeat well under Lease). If a
# worker crashes mid-job, the job becomes claimable again ~LeaseSeconds later
# — it never gets stuck. Cooperative cancellation + coarse progress are stored
# on the job row and exposed via `jobs list` (counts + safe summary only).
#
# Small-server guidance: keep MaxConcurrentJobs=1 (one job at a time, the
# default and current behaviour) so a single box never runs several heavy jobs
# at once; run at most ONE worker. Destructive/heavy jobs (storage reconcile
# with DeleteOrphans, backfills) stay opt-in and are enqueued explicitly.
#
# Jobs__WorkerEnabled=false
# Jobs__PollIntervalSeconds=10
# Jobs__BatchSize=10
# Jobs__DefaultMaxAttempts=3
# Jobs__RetryDelaySeconds=60
# Jobs__LeaseSeconds=120
# Jobs__HeartbeatSeconds=30
# Jobs__MaxConcurrentJobs=1
# Jobs__StaleRunningMinutes=30
#
# --- Cooperative scheduler (scheduler v2) ---------------------------------
# Long maintenance jobs (e.g. media.derivatives.backfill) run in SLICES: each
# slice processes a bounded amount of work, checkpoints, and yields so a queued
# foreground import (admin / staging-to-library) can run next. The most a
# foreground job waits behind a maintenance job is therefore ONE slice budget,
# not the whole backfill. Foreground jobs are not sliced (they self-pace).
# See docs/job-scheduling.md.
# Jobs__MaintenanceSliceSeconds=30       # wall-clock budget per maintenance slice
# Jobs__MaintenanceSliceItemBudget=200   # item budget per maintenance slice
# Jobs__ContinuationDelaySeconds=0       # delay before a yielded job's next slice
# Jobs__StarvationGraceSeconds=300       # a lower-priority job waiting this long
#                                        # earns ONE slice even under foreground load
# Jobs__MaxSlicesPerJob=0                # 0 = unlimited; backstop vs a looping job

# === Admin server-side import (slice 81, opt-in, OFF by default) ======
# Lets an ADMIN import files that already live on the SERVER filesystem
# (under a whitelisted root) into a chosen user's NubArca library,
# recreating the directory structure as logical folders. This is NOT
# browser upload and NOT a general filesystem browser: imports are bounded
# to the configured roots, symlinks are never followed, and NubArca's own
# storage/derived directories are rejected.
#
# Disabled unless AdminImport__Enabled=true AND at least one root is set.
# Each root must be an absolute path the api container can read (mount it
# read-only, e.g. `-v /mnt/raid1/incoming:/import/incoming:ro`).
#
# Imports run as `admin.import` background jobs. Because the in-process
# worker is OFF by default (see Background jobs above), a queued import only
# executes when EITHER Jobs__WorkerEnabled=true OR the operator runs
# `dotnet NubArca.Api.dll jobs run-once`. Enable the worker for hands-off
# imports.
#
# AdminImport__Enabled=false
# AdminImport__Roots__0=/import/incoming
# AdminImport__Roots__1=/import/archive
#
# --- Throttling (applies ONLY to admin import jobs, never to user uploads) ---
# A manual `jobs run-once` import once saturated a small server for hours.
# These knobs keep imports low-impact. Defaults below = off/unlimited except a
# cooperative scheduler yield; tune them DOWN on a small home server.
#
#   DelayBetweenFilesMs  sleep after each file (smooths CPU/I-O). Default 0.
#                        Recommended on a small server: 25–100.
#   MaxBytesPerSecond    cap the source read rate per file (bytes/sec).
#                        Default 0 = unlimited. Example: 20971520 (20 MiB/s).
#   MaxRunMinutes        per-slice wall-clock budget; when hit, the run is
#                        persisted + re-queued and RESUMES from its persisted
#                        item manifest (completed items are skipped by state).
#                        Default 0 = unlimited. Example: 30.
#   YieldEveryFiles      yield to the scheduler + flush progress every N files
#                        so the API/web stays responsive. Default 64 (min 1).
#
# AdminImport__DelayBetweenFilesMs=0
# AdminImport__MaxBytesPerSecond=0
# AdminImport__MaxRunMinutes=0
# AdminImport__YieldEveryFiles=64
#
# --- Massive-import optimisation (slice 92) ---
#   GenerateDerivativesInline  false (default) = the import does NOT generate
#                              image thumbnails inline; a media.derivatives
#                              backfill job is enqueued at the end of the run
#                              and gallery endpoints lazy-generate on demand.
#                              true = old behaviour (thumbnail per file at
#                              ingest time; slower massive imports).
#   ScanBatchSize              scan phase: persist discovered manifest items in
#                              batches of N. Default 500.
#   ItemBatchSize              import phase: page pending manifest items in
#                              batches of N (bounds memory). Default 200.
#
# AdminImport__GenerateDerivativesInline=false
# AdminImport__ScanBatchSize=500
# AdminImport__ItemBatchSize=200
#
# --- Metadata pipeline V2 (slice 94) ---
#   ExtractMetadataInline      false (default) = imports write only cheap
#                              detection facts inline (content type,
#                              dimensions, media category) and defer the full
#                              embedded EXIF/IPTC/XMP/GPS extraction to the
#                              metadata.embedded.backfill job enqueued at the
#                              end of the run (which also recomputes the
#                              gallery "date taken" and the owner-private GPS
#                              projection). true = extract at ingest time.
#
# AdminImport__ExtractMetadataInline=false
#
# For OS-level deprioritisation, run the manual worker low-priority:
#   nice -n 15 ionice -c3 dotnet NubArca.Api.dll jobs run-once
# (CPU niceness 15 + best-effort idle I-O; combine with the knobs above.)

# === Web remote-staging upload (slice 93) =========================
# Resumable browser chunk uploads into TEMPORARY staging space, verified and
# then imported through the admin-import pipeline. Staging is NOT NubArca
# blob storage: files become visible only after the import succeeds, and the
# staging root must not overlap Storage__RootPath. OFF by default.
#
# Lifecycle: create session → submit manifest → upload chunks (resumable;
# the server's missing-chunks endpoint is the source of truth) → verify →
# import (background job) → cleanup (configured sweeper or manual delete).
#
# Limitations to set expectations with users:
#   * a browser upload cannot continue while the browser/device is suspended
#     (mobile standby interrupts it; resume picks up the missing chunks);
#   * resuming after a reload may require re-selecting the same files/folder
#     (matched by relative path + size + modification time);
#   * staging temporarily uses extra disk space until imported + cleaned.
#
#   Enabled                 master switch. Default false.
#   RootPath                staging directory (per-session subdirs). In Docker
#                           mount a volume, e.g. /var/lib/nubarca/staging.
#   MaxSessionBytes         per-session byte ceiling. Default 64 GiB.
#   MaxFileBytes            per-file ceiling; the EFFECTIVE limit is
#                           min(this, Storage__MaxUploadBytes) so staging never
#                           accepts a file the import would reject. Default 2 GiB.
#   MaxFilesPerSession      manifest file-count ceiling. Default 50000.
#   Min/Max/DefaultChunkSizeBytes  chunking. The server picks the chunk size at
#                           manifest time (Default clamped into [Min, Max]);
#                           8 MiB default — keep under proxy/Kestrel body
#                           limits (Uploads__MaxRequestBodySizeBytes).
#   SessionTtlHours         sessions expire after this. Default 72.
#   CleanupEnabled          enables the background sweeper (expires overdue
#                           sessions, reclaims terminal ones past expiry) AND
#                           auto-cleanup of staging after a FULLY successful
#                           import. Default false; deletion is then manual.
#   CleanupIntervalMinutes  sweeper cadence. Default 60.
#
# Recommended small-server defaults: keep chunk size at 8 MiB, the UI uploads
# at most 3 chunks in parallel, and enable CleanupEnabled so staging space is
# reclaimed automatically.
#
# Staging__Enabled=false
# Staging__RootPath=/var/lib/nubarca/staging
# Staging__MaxSessionBytes=68719476736
# Staging__MaxFileBytes=2147483648
# Staging__MaxFilesPerSession=50000
# Staging__MinChunkSizeBytes=1048576
# Staging__MaxChunkSizeBytes=67108864
# Staging__DefaultChunkSizeBytes=8388608
# Staging__SessionTtlHours=72
# Staging__CleanupEnabled=false
# Staging__CleanupIntervalMinutes=60

# === First-run user provisioning ==================================
# NubArca has no public registration. The Seed:User:* path only runs when
# ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Development — in production it is a no-op even if
# set. The supported production path is the operator CLI:
#
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml --env-file .env run --rm api \
#     dotnet NubArca.Api.dll users ensure
#
# The CLI reads these three variables (or the equivalent --email /
# --display-name / --password flags). Pass them via `--env-file` rather than
# the command line so the password does not end up in your shell history.
#
# After the first user exists, leave these variables COMMENTED OUT. The api
# service should not have NUBARCA_ADMIN_PASSWORD in its environment at
# steady state; the CLI is a one-shot, not a service.
# NUBARCA_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com
# NUBARCA_ADMIN_DISPLAY_NAME=Admin
# NUBARCA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# Set to true to overwrite an existing user's password on the next
# `users ensure` run. Leave unset to make the call idempotent.
# NUBARCA_ADMIN_UPDATE_PASSWORD=false
# Set to true to grant the admin marker on the next `users ensure` run.
# Required for the FIRST user so they can reach /api/admin/*. Once granted,
# admin status is sticky — leave this unset on subsequent runs.
# NUBARCA_ADMIN_IS_ADMIN=false

# (Legacy dev-only Seed:User:* keys — left here only for clarity. They are
# IGNORED when ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production.)
# Seed__User__Email=
# Seed__User__DisplayName=
# Seed__User__Password=

# === Public installation origin ===
# This installation's public https origin, exactly as clients reach it (no
# trailing slash). It is deliberately NOT a source constant:
# installation-specific host is deployment configuration.
#
# Required, and FAIL-CLOSED, for anything that pins where a client talks to:
#   * a production NubArca TV build (tv/app.config.js) refuses to build without it
#     rather than embedding a fallback — a release APK once shipped pointing at a
#     LAN dev default and could never reach a server;
#   * deploy/publish-tv-apk.sh refuses to publish an APK whose embedded update URL
#     is not on this origin.
NUBARCA_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://nubarca.example.com

# === TV APK distribution host configuration ===
# Host directory the signed TV APK is published into, bind-mounted read-only at
# /download/tv in the frontend container.
#
# REQUIRED and fail-closed: Compose refuses to start the frontend when it is
# unset. There is deliberately no default, because a default would be one
# installation's directory and would silently publish to, or serve from, the
# wrong place on every other installation.
NUBARCA_TV_APK_DIR=/srv/nubarca/tv-apk
NUBARCA_TV_OTA_STORAGE_ROOT=/srv/nubarca/tv-updates
NUBARCA_TV_OTA_CERTIFICATE=/srv/nubarca/tv-ota-trust/certificate.pem
NUBARCA_TV_NODE=/usr/bin/node

# === TV OTA — HOST-SIDE RELEASE TOOLING ===
# Absolute host paths. These values are used by the publisher and are not passed
# to the API container. Keep the private key readable only by the release
# operator. The Android APK keystore is separate and is never an OTA input.
TV_OTA_STORAGE_ROOT=/absolute/host/path/tv-updates
NUBARCA_TV_OTA_CERTIFICATE=/absolute/host/path/certificate.pem
TV_OTA_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH=/absolute/host/path/private-key.pem

# === TV OTA — API CONTAINER PATHS ===
# Bind the host publication storage and public certificate file read-only to
# these paths in docker-compose.prod.local.yml. Never mount the OTA private key
# or Android release keystore into the API container.
TvUpdates__RootPath=/var/lib/nubarca/tv-updates
TvUpdates__CodeSigningCertificatePath=/var/lib/nubarca/tv-ota-trust/certificate.pem

# In-process OpenVINO acceleration (openvino-direct) — composed via
# docker-compose.facedirect-api.yml (api + worker, pinned images):
# FACE_DIRECT_IMAGE=nubarca-api:siglip-direct-<shortsha>
# WORKER_DIRECT_IMAGE=nubarca-worker:siglip-direct-<shortsha>
# OPENVINO_AI_MODEL_DIR=/srv/ai-models
# `stat -c %g /dev/dri/renderD128` on the target host:
# OPENVINO_RENDER_GID=109
# Per-model devices (defaults shown; FP32 everywhere):
# NUBARCA_FACE_DETECTOR_DEVICE=GPU
# NUBARCA_FACE_RECOGNIZER_DEVICE=CPU
# NUBARCA_PHOTO_TEXT_DEVICE=CPU
# NUBARCA_WORKER_FACE_DETECTOR_DEVICE=GPU
# NUBARCA_WORKER_FACE_RECOGNIZER_DEVICE=GPU
# NUBARCA_WORKER_PHOTO_IMAGE_DEVICE=CPU
# OPENVINO_QUERY_PHOTO_TEXT_DEVICE=GPU
# OPENVINO_WORKER_PHOTO_IMAGE_DEVICE=DUAL:CPU,GPU
# OPENVINO_WORKER_PHOTO_IMAGE_CONCURRENCY=2
# OPENVINO_WORKER_PHOTO_IMAGE_PERFORMANCE_MODE=LATENCY
# OPENVINO_WORKER_PHOTO_IMAGE_NUM_REQUESTS=0

# --- Optional Help assistant ------------------------------------------------
#
# An assistant that explains NubArca as a PRODUCT, through a model endpoint
# speaking the OpenAI-compatible Chat Completions format. See
# docs/help-assistant.md for the trust model, the privacy boundary, and what it
# can and cannot see.
#
# OFF unless it is enabled AND names a complete model profile: a half
# configuration leaves the feature disabled rather than half working.
#
# These reach the API container ONLY. The worker runs no Help endpoint and has
# no reason to hold a third-party credential.
#
# NEVER COMMIT A REAL KEY. This file is an example that lives in the repository;
# the key belongs in the deployment's own .env, which does not.
#
# TRUST IS EXPLICIT, and NubArca never guesses it from the URL. `localhost`, an
# RFC1918 address and a Docker service name are all things a reverse proxy in
# front of a cloud API also looks like, and a trusted GPU server on another host
# is none of them. An unknown, empty or numeric value is invalid — never
# "probably local".
#
#   External      the question leaves this installation. https:// and an API key
#                 are both required: the key travels in an Authorization header.
#   LocalTrusted  you are asserting the endpoint is yours. http:// is allowed and
#                 the API key is optional, because that is what an operator's own
#                 Ollama/vLLM/llama.cpp server usually is.
#
# There is deliberately no "allow insecure URL" switch. A plaintext endpoint is
# expressed as Trust=LocalTrusted, which says who holds the bytes rather than
# punching a hole in a statement about TLS.
#
# An external provider:
# Assistant__Enabled=false
# Assistant__HelpModel=help-default
# Assistant__Models__help-default__Protocol=OpenAiCompatible
# Assistant__Models__help-default__Trust=External
# Assistant__Models__help-default__BaseUrl=https://your-provider.example
# Assistant__Models__help-default__ApiKey=
# Assistant__Models__help-default__Model=
# Assistant__Models__help-default__Label=External AI
# Assistant__Models__help-default__TimeoutSeconds=30
# Assistant__Models__help-default__MaxOutputTokens=800
#
# …or your own endpoint on the container network. Note this still answers from
# public product documentation only: a trusted model is ELIGIBLE for more, and
# no feature uses that eligibility yet.
# Assistant__Enabled=false
# Assistant__HelpModel=local
# Assistant__Models__local__Protocol=OpenAiCompatible
# Assistant__Models__local__Trust=LocalTrusted
# Assistant__Models__local__BaseUrl=http://your-model-host:11434
# Assistant__Models__local__Model=
# Assistant__Models__local__Label=Local AI
#
# Where the pre-built public product-help corpus lives inside the image. The
# default is correct for the shipped image; override it only for a local run.
# Assistant__Help__CorpusPath=help-corpus.json
#
# LEGACY, kept so an installation configured before named profiles keeps
# working. It is adapted into ONE profile, always classified External — a
# configuration shape that predates the trust axis cannot assert one — and only
# when no Assistant__* value is set. Migrate to the block above.
# ExternalHelp__Enabled=false
# ExternalHelp__BaseUrl=https://your-provider.example
# ExternalHelp__ApiKey=
# ExternalHelp__Model=
# ExternalHelp__ProviderLabel=External AI
# ExternalHelp__TimeoutSeconds=30
# ExternalHelp__MaxOutputTokens=800

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local retrieval substrate (RAG)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# NubArca answers product questions from a LOCAL index. Lexical retrieval is
# always available and is a complete configuration on its own — the Product Help
# corpus ships inside the image, so nothing below is required for Help to work.
#
# These settings turn on the optional SEMANTIC accelerator: local ONNX text
# embeddings plus pgvector, fused with the lexical results. Every failure mode —
# disabled, no profile, missing model, no pgvector, unsupported dimension —
# falls back to lexical and reports a reason. None of them makes NubArca
# unhealthy, and none of them sends text to a hosted embedding service, because
# there is no such code path.
#
# Rag__SemanticEnabled=false
#
# The embedding profile, by its stable key. Explicit rather than "the newest
# model installed": a different model is a different coordinate system, so
# changing it means a new profile and a reindex, never a reinterpretation of
# vectors that already exist. Seed the profiles with `rag seed-profiles`.
#
#   rag-text-multilingual-e5-small-v1   local ONNX, 384 dimensions
#   rag-text-deterministic-v1           dev/test only; NOT a semantic model
#
# Rag__TextEmbeddingProfileKey=
#
# The model files themselves are operator configuration and are never
# downloaded: place them under Ai__Onnx__ModelDir/multilingual-e5-small/ as
# model.onnx + tokenizer.json, then check with `rag validate-model`.
#
# Server-side bounds. A deployment may make these TIGHTER; there is no value
# that removes one.
# Rag__MaxQueryCharacters=400
# Rag__MaxLexicalCandidates=60
# Rag__MaxVectorCandidates=60
# Rag__MaxFusedCandidates=60
# Rag__MaxIndexedChunks=200000
#
# Cosine floor at which a purely semantic hit counts as strong evidence on its
# own. Deliberately high: cosine is not calibrated across checkpoints, so the
# evidence decision stays anchored on the lexical gate.
# Rag__MinimumVectorScore=0.80
